  # Hardening params below are Stage 1's actual result: best of 10
  # evaluations (obj=0.0360), Dakota's search was manually halted there
  # since gains had clearly plateaued (eval 9 of the planned 25 -- see
  # CLAUDE.md). Only InitialYieldStress moved meaningfully from the old
  # Bond-Associated-tuned warm start (9.27e9 -> 9.77e9, +5.4%).
  #
  # DamageParameter1/2/3 bounds/warm-start are informed by Behzadinasab &
  # Foster's SFC3 paper (same Peridigm/Dakota workflow, same power-law
  # hardening formula, same Johnson Cook damage formula) -- their calibrated
  # d1=0.04, d2=2.5, d3=3 and search bounds d1[0.01,0.6] d2[0.1,4] d3[0.1,5]
  # showed our own D2 upper bound (0.5) had been far too tight, and that D3
  # should stay non-negative (our own negative-D3 excursions under
  # power-law were non-physical and caused the Stage 2/3 NaN crashes -- see
  # CLAUDE.md). Warm-started from the original informed guess
  # (D1=0.30, D2=0.04, D3=0.3), not the negative values found under
  # power-law, which are a numerically fragile dead end specific to that
  # hardening law's shape deficiency.
  #
  # ThresholdDamage is fixed (continuous_state, not searched) near 1.0 to
  # approximate a hard bond-cutoff damage function (see
  # driver_HE0_voce_hypoelastic_stage2_damage.sh for the rationale) --
  # Voce's own necking already supplies gradual pre-fracture softening, so
  # Johnson Cook doesn't need a wide ramp to do that job too. First attempt
  # used 0.97 (a near-instant cutoff) and crashed at eval 4/5 with
  # "isotropic_hardening_correspondence.cxx::radialReturnSaturationExponential:
  # Too many iterations in the plastic radial return" -- the near-simultaneous
  # loss of strength across many bonds at once was abrupt enough to push the
  # Voce plasticity model's local return-mapping iteration outside its
  # convergence region. 0.85 still gives a much narrower ramp than the
  # original wide-ramp default (0.5) while leaving the solver enough room to
  # converge normally (comfortably below the template's fixed Critical
  # Damage = 0.999).
  #
  # DamageParameter2's lower_bounds was also fixed here: it was previously
  # 0.05, above the intended 0.04 initial_point, so Dakota silently clamped
  # the warm start up to 0.05 on the first attempt. Now 0.02, matching
  # DamageParameter1's lower bound, so the true warm start is honored.
  #
  # Second attempt (this one): the first attempt's best result (eval 8,
  # D1=0.30, D2=0.04, D3=0.55) produced a real fracture, but late -- 18.07%
  # vs real 15.37%. Root cause: narrowing the damage ramp (ThresholdDamage
  # 0.5 -> 0.85) removed the broad pre-failure softening a wide ramp
  # provides (bonds start losing stiffness as soon as D crosses threshold,
  # well before any single bond fully breaks, which helps the aggregate
  # force drop earlier). With a narrow ramp, aggregate force stays near its
  # undamaged value until individual points suddenly, almost
  # simultaneously, lose all strength -- so the same D1/D2/D3 now needs
  # more accumulated strain to produce a visible macroscopic drop. Pulling
  # D1 (and D2) down lowers epsF directly, forcing local failure -- and
  # therefore the cascading macroscopic drop -- to happen sooner, to
  # compensate. D3 kept at this stage's own best-found value (0.55) since
  # this correction targets D1/D2 specifically.
  # Seventh attempt (this one): 0.85 with the tolerance fix converged
  # cleanly across 30 evals with no crash -- best result eval 15 (D1=0.1355,
  # D2=0.03, D3=0.55, obj=0.0413, fracture 15.27% vs real 15.37%, only 0.10
  # points off, the best fracture-timing match of the whole project). But
  # visual inspection still showed a small disturbance ~13.3% strain before
  # the big oscillation at the actual fracture point (15.27%). Since a
  # HIGHER threshold keeps bonds fully intact longer before any local
  # softening begins (the opposite of what a wide/low ramp does -- see the
  # attempt 1->2 note above on why low thresholds pull fracture earlier),
  # pushing ThresholdDamage up further should tighten that gap rather than
  # widen it. Warm-started from eval 15's D1/D2/D3 (this attempt's own best
  # result), moving ThresholdDamage from 0.85 to 0.95 -- a step short of
  # the 0.97 that crashed before the tolerance fix, still to be validated
  # at this level with the fix in place.
  # Eighth attempt (this one): 0.95 also converged with no crash -- best
  # result eval 4 (D1=0.1355, D2=0.03, D3=1.05, obj=0.0734, fracture 15.70%
  # vs real 15.37%). Visual inspection: eval 3 (a different D1/D2/D3, same
  # 0.95 threshold) showed literally zero pre-fracture oscillation out to
  # ~21% strain, but eval 4 still had a small disturbance ~14% -- confirming
  # the specific damage-parameter combination matters some too, not just
  # the threshold, though threshold remains the dominant lever. Pushing to
  # 0.97 next (the value that crashed pre-fix) to test with the tolerance
  # fix in place, warm-started from eval 4's D1/D2/D3.
  continuous_design = 3
    descriptors                    'DamageParameter1'  'DamageParameter2'  'DamageParameter3'
    initial_point                   0.1355              0.03                1.05
    lower_bounds                    0.02                0.02                0.0
    upper_bounds                    0.60                4.00                5.0
  # Third attempt: 0.85 also crashed with the same
  # "Too many iterations in the plastic radial return" error, this time at
  # eval 4/5 with different D1/D2/D3 combinations than the 0.97 crash --
  # confirming this instability is tied to ThresholdDamage's abruptness
  # itself, not the specific damage-parameter values. Backed off further to
  # 0.7 (fourth attempt) -- crashed again at eval 9/10. Its best result
  # (eval 1, unperturbed warm start) actually produced an excellent
  # fracture match (15.12% vs real 15.37%), but visual inspection showed
  # the wild post-fracture-style oscillation actually starting *before*
  # the detected fracture point -- a few individual points snap almost
  # instantly once they cross the narrow threshold, injecting noise before
  # enough of the cross-section has failed to trigger the global
  # fracture-strain detector. Fifth attempt: went back to the original
  # wide-ramp default (0.5) -- crashed too, at eval 4, proving the crash was
  # never actually about ramp width at all (0.5 is the exact value every
  # power-law run used without ever hitting this error). Root cause fixed
  # directly instead: radialReturnSaturationExponential's Newton-iteration
  # convergence tolerance was a pure absolute value pegged to bulkMod,
  # unreachable once a damage-driven abrupt stiffness loss produces an
  # unusually large trial stress. Added a relative-tolerance fallback
  # (see isotropic_hardening_correspondence.cxx) so the solver can still
  # converge in that regime -- rebuilt into peridigm-masoud:voce-hypoelastic.
  # Sixth attempt (this one): back to 0.85 (the exact value that crashed
  # twice before the fix) to directly verify the fix resolves it, now that
  # ramp width can go back to being chosen on fit/shape grounds rather than
  # numerical-stability grounds.
  continuous_state = 5
    descriptors                    'InitialYieldStress'  'SaturatedYieldStress'  'ExponentialConstant'  'LinearConstant'  'ThresholdDamage'
    initial_state                   9.77e9                 1.35e10                 22.0                   2.0e8             0.97

